            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-xxl",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-xl",
            "max_width_desktop": "135ch",
            "richtext": "<p>Shopping for an air purifier fan combo feels like a smart compromise. Why buy two devices when one handles both cooling and air cleaning?</p><p>But here's the honest truth: understanding what you're actually getting matters more than convenience. Combo units work well for tiny spaces and tight budgets, but dedicated air purifiers significantly outperform them. Let's explore when each makes sense.</p><h2>What Is an Air Purifier Fan Combo Device?</h2><p>An air purifier fan combo combines filtration with cooling circulation in one device. Most use HEPA filters while providing fan airflow. Prices range from $100 to over $700.</p><p>One device saves space and requires only one outlet. But performance tradeoffs exist and they're significant.</p><h2>Air Purifier Fan Combo Performance vs Dedicated Units</h2><p>Here's what testing consistently shows: combo units are adequate fans with decent air cleaning, but significantly less effective than dedicated purifiers at improving air quality.</p><p>Why? Design priorities. Combo units balance fan blades, cooling airflow, and filter positioning. Dedicated purifiers optimize solely for air cleaning.</p><p>This matters when you look at the Clean Air Delivery Rate (CADR) , the objective measure of how much clean air a device produces per minute. A combo at any price typically delivers lower CADR than a dedicated purifier at the same cost. That means slower cleaning and longer wait times.</p><h2>When an Air Purifier Fan Combo Actually Makes Sense</h2>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "s3"
          }
        },
        "image_caption_KnqYBR": {
