    "article_content_CXmqDG": {
      "type": "article__content",
      "blocks": {
        "text_CrfwFz": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-0",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "80ch",
            "richtext": "<p>h2 Pet allergies</p><p>p Do you have dog, cat or other pet allergies? Did you know that most pet allergies are not caused by pet hair, but by the dander your cat, dog, or other pet produces? Pet dander is made up of small flecks of skin shed by cats, dogs, rodents, birds and other animals with fur or feathers. Pet-related allergy triggers or allergens also come from proteins found in the pets’ saliva, urine, and feces. The obvious way to protect your indoor air quality is to not have a pet in your home, of course. But if going petless isn’t an option:</p><ul><li>Keep pets out of the bedroom of the allergy sufferer.</li><li>Keep pets off furniture, especially upholstery. </li><li>Keep pets away from carpeted areas. </li><li>Vacuum carpets and furniture to reduce the amount of dander.</li></ul>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
        "product_desc_image_hapr9i": {
          "type": "product_desc_image",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-md",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-xxxl",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "80ch",
            "color": "bg-gray-athans text-blue",
            "title": "",
            "title_style": "p1",
            "product": "1342-master-product",
            "btn_text": "Shop Now",
            "btn_style": "btn"
          }
        },
        "text_KU8fxJ": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "margin_top_mobile": "mt-lg",
            "margin_bottom_mobile": "mb-0",
            "padding_top_mobile": "pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_mobile": "pb-0",
            "margin_top_desktop": "desktop:mt-xl",
            "margin_bottom_desktop": "desktop:mb-0",
            "padding_top_desktop": "desktop:pt-0",
            "padding_bottom_desktop": "desktop:pb-0",
            "max_width_desktop": "80ch",
            "richtext": "<h2>h2 Dust Mites</h2><p>Dust mites, another cause of indoor allergies, are microscopic arthropods that feed primarily on flakes of human skin that are shed around the home. And dust mites are a year-round allergy trigger. They may be more common during the humid summer months, but they also get stirred up into the air when you start up your indoor heating in autumn. The best natural remedies center on preventing exposure to the critters, so to reduce dust mite allergy symptoms:</p><h3>H3 Do you have dog, cat or other pet allergies?</h3><ul><li>Before you turn the central heating on for the first time after summer, clean the air vents throughout the house. Allergens can get trapped in the vents and will fill the air as soon as you start the furnace.</li><li>Dust mites love bedrooms! Cover your mattress and pillows in dust-proof covers, and regularly wash all bedding in hot water.</li><li>Dust and vacuum your home regularly. Be sure to wear a filtering mask while cleaning.</li><li>Consider replacing wall-to-wall carpet with hardwood floors.</li></ul>",
            "richtext_alignment": "text-left",
            "richtext_style": "p2"
          }
        },
